<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Wrekin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wrekin is an isolated hill in east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, rising to 407 metres above the Shropshire plain. Its summit carries a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hillfort, thought to have been a chief centre of the Cornovii before the Roman conquest. The Roman city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viroconium-cornoviorum">Viroconium</a> was built at Wroxeter below it, and the hill remains a well-known landmark of the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-wrekin.jpg" alt="The Wrekin" width="309" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rhodian at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rhekin_by_Wrodian.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
